<h2 id=intro>Introduction</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

Many forms collect a single value through several small text fields: a telephone number split
into area code, central office code, and line number; a six-digit verification code with one box
per digit; a card number in groups of four. Every one of these forms currently needs script to:

* move focus to the next field once the current one is full;
* move back to the previous field when the user presses Backspace at the start of a field;
* let the arrow keys move the caret from one field into the next;
* spread pasted or autofilled text across the fields instead of cutting it off at the first
    field's <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#attr-input-maxlength"><code>maxlength</code></a>; and
* put the pieces back together for the server, usually by copying them into a hidden field.

These scripts are rewritten for countless sites, behave slightly differently on each one, and
often break input methods, autofill, and assistive technology.

The <{input/link}> attribute lets authors declare the relationship instead, and leaves the behavior
to the user agent.

<div class=example id=example-intro-phone>
    A form asks for a North American telephone number in three parts:

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <form action="/register">
      <fieldset>
        <legend>Phone number</legend>
        <input type="tel" id="areaCode" name="phoneNumber" maxlength="3"
               link="centralOfficeCode" autocomplete="tel-area-code" aria-label="Area code">
        <input type="tel" id="centralOfficeCode" name="phoneNumber" maxlength="3"
               link="lineNumber" autocomplete="tel-local-prefix" aria-label="Central office code">
        <input type="tel" id="lineNumber" name="phoneNumber" maxlength="4"
               autocomplete="tel-local-suffix" aria-label="Line number">
      </fieldset>
      <button>Register</button>
    </form>
    </xmp>

    The user types <kbd>5551234567</kbd> into the first field. After the third digit, focus moves to
    <code>centralOfficeCode</code>; after the sixth, it moves to <code>lineNumber</code>. Submitting the
    form sends one entry:

    <pre>phoneNumber=5551234567</pre>

    Without the <{input/link}> attributes, the same form would send three entries:
    <code>phoneNumber=555&amp;phoneNumber=123&amp;phoneNumber=4567</code>.
</div>

Two attributes do two separate jobs:

* <{input/link}> decides where focus and text go next.
* <{input/name}> decides what is submitted together: linked controls that share a name are
    submitted as one entry, with values concatenated in link order.

User agents that don't support this specification still show separate, working fields, and
submit each field as its own entry.


<h2 id=link-attribute>The <code>link</code> attribute</h2>

<h3 id=linkable-elements>Linkable elements</h3>

A <dfn export>linkable element</dfn> is either:

* an <{input}> element whose <{input/type}> attribute is in the
    <a element-state for=input>Text</a>, <a element-state for=input>Search</a>,
    <a element-state for=input>Telephone</a>, or <a element-state for=input>Password</a> state; or
* a <{select}> element that does not have a <{select/multiple}> attribute.

Note: Controls that aren't used to enter a piece of a larger value, such as checkboxes and radio
buttons, are not linkable. <{select}> elements with a <{select/multiple}> attribute are excluded
because they can contribute more than one value.

<h3 id=the-link-content-attribute>The content attribute</h3>

The <dfn element-attr for="input, select">link</dfn> content attribute identifies the control that
comes next in a sequence of controls that together hold a single value. It can be specified on
<{input}> and <{select}> elements.

If present, the attribute's value must be the [=Element/ID=] of a [=linkable element=] in the same
[=tree=] as the element, other than the element itself. The attribute must only be specified on
linkable elements. On any other <{input}> or <{select}> element, the attribute does not apply and
has no effect.

Authors must not specify <{input/link}> attributes such that two elements have the same
[=link target=], or such that following link targets from an element eventually leads back to that
element.

<h3 id=the-link-idl-attribute>The IDL attribute</h3>

<xmp class=idl>
interface mixin LinkedControlAttributes {
  [CEReactions, Reflect="link"] attribute Element? linkElement;
};
HTMLInputElement includes LinkedControlAttributes;
HTMLSelectElement includes LinkedControlAttributes;
</xmp>

The <dfn attribute for=LinkedControlAttributes>linkElement</dfn> IDL attribute must
[=reflect=] the <{input/link}> content attribute.

Note: Like {{HTMLButtonElement/commandForElement}}, {{LinkedControlAttributes/linkElement}} returns
the element that the attribute refers to, and can be set to an element that has no ID. Setting it
sets the content attribute to the empty string.

<div class=example id=example-idl>
    <xmp highlight=js>
    const [area, prefix, line] = form.querySelectorAll('input[name="phoneNumber"]');

    // Build the chain from script. None of these elements need an id.
    area.linkElement = prefix;
    prefix.linkElement = line;

    area.linkElement === prefix;               // true
    new FormData(form).get("phoneNumber");     // e.g. "5551234567"

    // Feature detection.
    const supported = "linkElement" in HTMLInputElement.prototype;
    </xmp>
</div>

<h3 id=link-chains>Link targets and link chains</h3>

<div algorithm>
    The <dfn export>link target</dfn> of an element |element| is the result of these steps:

    1. If |element| is not a [=linkable element=], then return null.

    1. Let |target| be the result of running |element|'s
        <a spec=html for=Element lt="get the attr-associated element">get the
        <code>link</code>-associated element</a>.

    1. If any of the following are true, then return null:
        * |target| is null;
        * |target| is |element|;
        * |target| is not a [=linkable element=]; or
        * |target|'s [=tree/root=] is not |element|'s [=tree/root=].

    1. Return |target|.
</div>

<div algorithm>
    The <dfn>link source</dfn> of an element |element| is the first [=linkable element=], in
    [=tree order=], among the [=tree/inclusive descendants=] of |element|'s [=tree/root=], whose
    [=link target=] is |element|. If there is no such element, the link source is null.
</div>

Note: When more than one element names the same link target, only the first of them in tree
order is linked to it. The others act as if they had no <{input/link}> attribute.

<div algorithm="next and previous linked controls">
    The <dfn>tentative next control</dfn> of an element |element| is |element|'s [=link target=],
    if that is not null and its [=link source=] is |element|. Otherwise, it is null.

    An element |element| is <dfn>in a link cycle</dfn> if, starting with |current| set to
    |element|'s [=tentative next control=] and then repeatedly setting |current| to |current|'s
    tentative next control, |current| eventually becomes |element|.

    Note: An element is the tentative next control of at most one other element, so this process
    always either comes back to |element| or reaches null.

    The <dfn export>next linked control</dfn> of an element |element| is null if |element| is
    [=in a link cycle=], and |element|'s [=tentative next control=] otherwise.

    The <dfn export>previous linked control</dfn> of an element |element| is null if |element| is
    [=in a link cycle=], and |element|'s [=link source=] otherwise.
</div>

Note: Controls that form a cycle aren't linked at all. That way, an authoring mistake gives
separate, ordinary controls instead of a chain with no clear start.

<div algorithm>
    The <dfn export>link chain</dfn> of a [=linkable element=] |element| is the [=list=] returned by
    these steps:

    1. Let |head| be |element|.

    1. [=iteration/While=] |head|'s [=previous linked control=] is not null, set |head| to |head|'s
        previous linked control.

    1. Let |chain| be « |head| ».

    1. Let |current| be |head|'s [=next linked control=].

    1. [=iteration/While=] |current| is not null:

        1. [=list/Append=] |current| to |chain|.

        1. Set |current| to |current|'s [=next linked control=].

    1. Return |chain|.
</div>

A <dfn export>linked control</dfn> is a [=linkable element=] whose [=link chain=]'s [=list/size=]
is greater than 1. A <dfn export>linked text control</dfn> is a [=linked control=] that is an
<{input}> element.

A [=linked control=] |control| is <dfn for="linked control">available</dfn> if |control| is
[=focusable=] and, if |control| is an <{input}> element, |control| is [=mutable=].

<div algorithm="next and previous available linked controls">
    The <dfn>next available linked control</dfn> of a [=linked control=] |control| is the result of
    these steps:

    1. Let |current| be |control|'s [=next linked control=].

    1. [=iteration/While=] |current| is not null and |current| is not
        [=linked control/available=], set |current| to |current|'s [=next linked control=].

    1. Return |current|.

    The <dfn>previous available linked control</dfn> of a [=linked control=] is found the same way,
    using [=previous linked controls=] instead of [=next linked controls=].
</div>

Note: Skipping controls that aren't available means a disabled, read-only, or hidden piece of a
value, such as a fixed country code, doesn't stop the user from moving through the rest.

<div class=example id=example-nonconforming>
    The following markup is non-conforming. User agents handle it as described in the comments.

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <!-- A cycle: neither control is linked. -->
    <input id="a" link="b" maxlength="2">
    <input id="b" link="a" maxlength="2">

    <!-- Two controls link to "z". Only "p", the first in tree order, is linked to it. -->
    <input id="p" link="z" maxlength="3">
    <input id="q" link="z" maxlength="3">
    <input id="z" maxlength="3">

    <!-- <textarea> is not a linkable element, so the link attribute is ignored. -->
    <input link="notes" maxlength="10">
    <textarea id="notes"></textarea>
    </xmp>
</div>


<h2 id=user-interaction>User interaction</h2>

The requirements in this section make a [=link chain=] behave, for editing, like one field divided
into segments. They apply only to actions the user takes, not to changes made by script (for
example, setting {{HTMLInputElement/value}} or calling
{{HTMLInputElement/setSelectionRange()}}).

<h3 id=advancing-focus>Advancing focus</h3>

<div algorithm>
    To <dfn>advance focus</dfn> from a [=linked control=] |control|:

    1. If the user has turned off automatic focus advancement (see [[#a11y]]), then return.

    1. Let |next| be |control|'s [=next available linked control=].

    1. If |next| is null, then return.

    1. Run the [=focusing steps=] for |next|.

    1. If |next| is an <{input}> element and is [=focused=], then [=set the selection range=] of
        |next| with 0 and the [=string/length=] of |next|'s [=form control/value=].
</div>

Note: Selecting the next control's contents means typing replaces whatever it already holds, as it
does when the user tabs into a text field on most platforms.

<div algorithm>
    To <dfn>check for automatic advance</dfn> for a [=linked text control=] |control|:

    1. If |control| is not [=focused=], then return.

    1. If |control| has no [=maximum allowed value length=], then return.

    1. If the [=string/length=] of |control|'s [=form control/value=] is less than |control|'s
        [=maximum allowed value length=], then return.

    1. If |control|'s [=text control/selection=] is not collapsed, or the
        [=text entry cursor position=] is not at the end of |control|'s
        [=form control/value=], then return.

    1. [=Advance focus=] from |control|.
</div>

Whenever the user agent queues a task to fire an <code>input</code> event at a [=linked text control=]
|control| because the user inserted text into it, it must then [=queue an element task=] on the
[=user interaction task source=] given |control| to [=check for automatic advance=] for |control|.
Inserting text includes typing, committing an input method composition, pasting, dropping, and
accepting a replacement suggestion, such as a spelling correction. It does not include autofill,
updates to an input method composition that is still in progress, or deleting text.

Note: The check runs after the <code>input</code> event has been dispatched. If an event listener
changes the control's value or moves focus, the check sees the result.

When the user picks an option of a [=linked control=] |control| that is a <{select}> element
from the element's [=select/drop-down box=], the user agent must, after the task queued by
[=send select update notifications=] has run, [=queue an element task=] on the
[=user interaction task source=] given |control| to run these steps:

1. If |control| is not [=focused=], then return.

1. [=Advance focus=] from |control|.

The user agent must not [=advance focus=] from a <{select}> element when the user changes its
selected option without picking it from the drop-down box. This covers using the arrow keys while
the drop-down box is closed, as some platforms allow, and a <{select}> element that is being
rendered as a [=select/list box=].

<h3 id=distributing-text>Distributing text</h3>

When the user inserts text into a [=linked text control=] |control|, and the insertion was not
canceled (for example, by canceling its <code>beforeinput</code> event [[UIEVENTS]]), the user agent
must run [=distribute text=] given |control|, the inserted text, and the start and end offsets of
the part of |control|'s [=form control/value=] that the insertion replaces. The inserted text is
taken after the processing the user agent normally applies to text inserted into |control|, such as
removing line breaks. The replaced part is the [=text control/selection=], or an empty range at the
[=text entry cursor position=] if the selection is collapsed. If [=distribute text=] returns false,
the user agent must insert the text as it normally would. Otherwise, the insertion has been fully
handled.

When the user agent autofills a [=linked text control=] |control| with a string that is longer
than |control|'s [=maximum allowed value length=], it must run [=distribute text=] given |control|,
the string, 0, the [=string/length=] of |control|'s [=form control/value=], and false, instead of
truncating the string.

<div algorithm>
    To <dfn>distribute text</dfn> given a [=linked text control=] |control|, a [=string=] |text|,
    integers |start| and |end|, and an optional boolean |move focus| (default true):

    1. If |control| has no [=maximum allowed value length=], then return false.

    1. Let |value| be |control|'s [=form control/value=].

    1. Let |before| be the [=code unit substring=] from 0 to |start| within |value|.

    1. Let |after| be the [=code unit substring=] from |end| to |value|'s [=string/length=] within
        |value|.

    1. If the sum of the [=string/lengths=] of |before|, |text|, and |after| is less than or equal to
        |control|'s [=maximum allowed value length=], then return false.

    1. Let |target| be |control|.

    1. Let |prefix| be |before|, let |suffix| be |after|, and let |remaining| be |text|.

    1. Let |changed| be an empty [=list=].

    1. Let |last target| be |control|, and let |caret| be the [=string/length=] of |before|.

    1. [=iteration/While=] true:

        1. Let |room| be |target|'s [=maximum allowed value length=] minus the [=string/length=] of
            |prefix|, or the [=string/length=] of |remaining| if |target| has no maximum allowed
            value length. If |room| is less than 0, set |room| to 0.

        1. Let |piece| be the [=code unit prefix=] of |remaining| whose [=string/length=] is the
            smaller of |room| and the [=string/length=] of |remaining|. If |piece| ends with a
            [=leading surrogate=] and |remaining| is longer than |piece|, remove that last
            [=code unit=] from |piece|.

        1. Set |remaining| to the [=code unit substring=] from the [=string/length=] of |piece| to the
            [=string/length=] of |remaining| within |remaining|.

        1. Let |excess| be the sum of the [=string/lengths=] of |prefix|, |piece|, and |suffix|,
            minus |target|'s [=maximum allowed value length=]. If |target| has no maximum allowed
            value length, or |excess| is less than 0, set |excess| to 0.

        1. Let |kept| be the [=code unit substring=] from the smaller of |excess| and the
            [=string/length=] of |suffix|, to the [=string/length=] of |suffix|, within |suffix|. If
            |kept| starts with a [=trailing surrogate=], remove that first [=code unit=] from
            |kept|.

        1. Let |new value| be the [=string/concatenation=] of « |prefix|, |piece|, |kept| ».

        1. If |new value| is not [=string/identical to=] |target|'s [=form control/value=], then:

            1. Set |target|'s [=form control/value=] to |new value|, and set its
                [=dirty value flag=] to true. The value counts as having last been changed by a user
                edit.

            1. [=list/Append=] |target| to |changed|.

        1. Set |last target| to |target|, and set |caret| to the sum of the [=string/lengths=] of
            |prefix| and |piece|.

        1. If |remaining| is the empty string, then [=iteration/break=].

        1. Let |next| be |target|'s [=next available linked control=].

        1. If any of the following are true, then [=iteration/break=]:
            * |next| is null;
            * |next| is not an <{input}> element; or
            * the <{input/type}> attribute of exactly one of |target| and |next| is in the
                <a element-state for=input>Password</a> state.

        1. Set |target| to |next|, set |prefix| to the empty string, and set |suffix| to |next|'s
            [=form control/value=].

    1. If |move focus| is true, then:

        1. If |last target| is not |control|, then run the [=focusing steps=] for |last target|.

        1. If |last target| is [=focused=], then [=set the selection range=] of |last target| with
            |caret| and |caret|.

    1. [=list/For each=] |changed control| of |changed|, [=queue an element task=] on the
        [=user interaction task source=] given |changed control| to run these steps:

        1. [=Fire an event=] named <code>input</code> at |changed control|, with the
            {{Event/bubbles}} and {{Event/composed}} attributes initialized to true.

        1. If |move focus| is false, or |changed control| is neither |control| nor
            |last target|, then set |changed control|'s [=user validity=] to true and [=fire an event=] named
            {{HTMLElement/change}} at |changed control|, with the {{Event/bubbles}} attribute
            initialized to true.

    1. Return true.
</div>

Note: Text that fits is inserted normally. When text doesn't fit, each control takes as much as it
can hold after the insertion point. To make room, it replaces only as many of the following
characters as it has to. Anything left over is inserted at the start of the next available linked
text control, under the same rule. This one rule covers pasting a whole value into the first
control, typing past the end of a full control into the next one, and typing over a full chain. Distribution
stops at a <{select}> element, at the end of the chain, and at any boundary between a
<a element-state for=input>Password</a> control and a control of another type. Text left over at
that point is discarded, as it would be by <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#attr-input-maxlength"><code>maxlength</code></a>.

Note: The <code>input</code> events fire in link order, after focus has moved. The control that
ends up focused, and the control the user started in, get their {{HTMLElement/change}} events in
the usual way, when they lose focus. Controls in between, which never have focus, get their
{{HTMLElement/change}} events straight away, as does every control changed by autofill.

<div class=example id=example-distribution>
    With the telephone number fields from <a href="#example-intro-phone">the introduction</a>:

    <table class=data>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>User action<th><code>areaCode</code><th><code>centralOfficeCode</code><th><code>lineNumber</code><th>Focus afterward
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>Type <kbd>555</kbd> into the empty <code>areaCode</code><td>555<td><td><td><code>centralOfficeCode</code>, contents selected
            <tr><td>Type <kbd>123</kbd><td>555<td>123<td><td><code>lineNumber</code>
            <tr><td>Press <kbd>Backspace</kbd> in the empty <code>lineNumber</code><td>555<td>12<td><td><code>centralOfficeCode</code>, caret at end
            <tr><td>Click at the end of <code>areaCode</code> and type <kbd>9</kbd><td>555<td>912<td><td><code>centralOfficeCode</code>, caret after 9
            <tr><td>Type <kbd>8</kbd><td>555<td>982<td><td><code>centralOfficeCode</code>, caret after 8
            <tr><td>Select all of <code>areaCode</code> and paste <kbd>5551234567</kbd><td>555<td>123<td>4567<td><code>lineNumber</code>, caret at end
            <tr><td>Paste <kbd>8005550199</kbd> with the caret at the start of <code>areaCode</code><td>800<td>555<td>0199<td><code>lineNumber</code>, caret at end
    </table>
</div>

<h3 id=moving-between-controls>Moving between linked text controls</h3>

When the user asks to delete the content just before the [=text entry cursor position=] (for
example, by pressing <kbd>Backspace</kbd>) in a [=linked text control=] |control| whose
[=text control/selection=] is collapsed at the start of its [=form control/value=], and the request
was not canceled, the user agent must run these steps instead of its usual behavior:

1. Let |previous| be |control|'s [=previous available linked control=].

1. If |previous| is null, then return.

1. Run the [=focusing steps=] for |previous|.

1. If |previous| is an <{input}> element and is [=focused=], then:

    1. Let |length| be the [=string/length=] of |previous|'s [=form control/value=].

    1. [=Set the selection range=] of |previous| with |length| and |length|.

    1. Carry out the user's deletion request in |previous|, as if the user had made it there. This
        includes firing the events such a deletion fires, and deleting as much text as the
        platform's conventions say (for example, one grapheme cluster).

When the user asks to move the [=text entry cursor position=] back by one position, without
extending the selection, in a [=linked text control=] |control| whose [=text control/selection=] is
collapsed at the start of its [=form control/value=], and the request was not canceled, the user
agent must run these steps instead of its usual behavior. An example of such a request is the
<kbd>←</kbd> key in a left-to-right control.

1. Let |previous| be |control|'s [=previous available linked control=].

1. If |previous| is null or is not an <{input}> element, then return and act as usual.

1. Run the [=focusing steps=] for |previous|.

1. If |previous| is [=focused=], then [=set the selection range=] of |previous| with |length| and
    |length|, where |length| is the [=string/length=] of |previous|'s [=form control/value=].

When the user asks to move the [=text entry cursor position=] forward by one position, without
extending the selection, in a [=linked text control=] |control| whose [=text control/selection=] is
collapsed at the end of its [=form control/value=], and the request was not canceled, the user
agent must run these steps instead of its usual behavior. An example of such a request is the
<kbd>→</kbd> key in a left-to-right control.

1. Let |next| be |control|'s [=next available linked control=].

1. If |next| is null or is not an <{input}> element, then return and act as usual.

1. Run the [=focusing steps=] for |next|.

1. If |next| is [=focused=], then [=set the selection range=] of |next| with 0 and 0.

Note: The arrow keys never move focus into or out of a <{select}> element, because they already
change the selected option there. Backspace can move focus back to a <{select}> element, but it
doesn't change the selection.

Note: The <code>keydown</code> event for these keys fires at the control where the user pressed the
key, before focus moves. Canceling it stops the behavior.


<h2 id=form-submission>Form submission</h2>

<h3 id=joined-groups>Joined groups</h3>

<div algorithm>
    The <dfn export>joined group</dfn> of a [=linked control=] |control| is the [=list=] of the
    [=list/items=] |member| of |control|'s [=link chain=], in order, for which all of the following
    are true:

    * |member| has a <{input/name}> attribute whose value is [=string/identical to=] the value of
        |control|'s <{input/name}> attribute;
    * |member|'s [=input/form owner=] is not null and is |control|'s [=input/form owner=];
    * |member| is not [=form control/disabled=]; and
    * |member| does not have a <{datalist}> element ancestor.
</div>

<div algorithm>
    The <dfn export>joined value</dfn> of a [=joined group=] |group| is the result of these steps:

    1. Let |value| be the empty string.

    1. [=list/For each=] |member| of |group|:

        1. If |member| is a <{select}> element, then, for each <{option}> element |option| in
            |member|'s [=list of options=] whose [=selectedness=] is true and that is not
            [=option/disabled=], append |option|'s [=option/value=] to |value|.

        1. Otherwise, append |member|'s [=form control/value=] to |value|.

    1. Return |value|.
</div>

Note: Only controls that are linked *and* share a name are joined. Controls that share a name
without being linked, such as a group of checkboxes, still submit separate entries. Linked controls
with different names still move focus from one to the next, but each name gets its own entry. A
disabled member contributes nothing, but doesn't split the group.

<h3 id=patch-construct-the-entry-list>Changes to constructing the entry list</h3>

The algorithm to [=constructing the entry list|construct the entry list=] [[!HTML]] is changed as
follows. Inserted text is <ins>marked like this</ins> and removed text is <del>marked like
this</del>. Steps that don't change are summarized in brackets.

<div algorithm="construct the entry list (patched)">
    5. For each element |field| in <var ignore>controls</var>, in [=tree order=]:

        1. [If any of the skip conditions are true, such as |field| being disabled, then continue.]

        1. [If |field| is an Image Button, then append its coordinates and continue.]

        1. [If |field| is a form-associated custom element, then perform its entry construction
            algorithm and continue.]

        1. If either the |field| element does not have a <{input/name}> attribute specified, or its
            <{input/name}> attribute's value is the empty string, then continue.

        1. Let |name| be the value of the |field| element's <{input/name}> attribute.

        1. <ins>If |field| is a [=linked control=] and the [=list/size=] of |field|'s
            [=joined group=] is greater than 1, then:</ins>

            1. <ins>If |field| is not the first [=list/item=] of |field|'s [=joined group=], then
                continue.</ins>

            1. <ins>[=entry list/Create an entry=] with |name| and the [=joined value=] of |field|'s
                [=joined group=], and append it to |entry list|.</ins>

        1. <del>If</del> <ins>Otherwise, if</ins> the |field| element is a <{select}> element, then
            for each <{option}> element in the <{select}> element's [=list of options=] whose
            [=selectedness=] is true and that is not [=option/disabled=], [=entry list/create an
            entry=] with |name| and the [=option/value=] of the <{option}> element, and append it to
            |entry list|.

        1. [The remaining "Otherwise, if" steps for checkboxes, radio buttons, file uploads,
            <code>_charset_</code>, and all other controls are unchanged.]

        1. [If the element has a <code>dirname</code> attribute, then append its directionality
            entry. This step is unchanged.]
</div>

Note: The joined entry is appended when the loop reaches the group's first member *in link order*.
Members that come later in the group are skipped, wherever they are in the tree. The
<code>dirname</code> step still runs for the first member, so a joined group contributes at most one
<code>dirname</code> entry.

Note: {{FormData}}'s constructor and the {{HTMLElement/formdata}} event use the same algorithm.
Scripts calling <code>new FormData(form)</code> therefore see the joined value, exactly as the
server does.

<h3 id=other-features>Interaction with other features</h3>

*This section is non-normative.*

: Constraint validation
:: Each control is validated on its own. Authors can use <{input/required}>,
    <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#attr-input-minlength"><code>minlength</code></a>, and <{input/pattern}> on each segment, for example
    <code>pattern="[0-9]{3}"</code>.
: <code>form.elements</code>
:: This specification doesn't change {{HTMLFormElement/elements}} or {{RadioNodeList}}. Each
    segment remains its own control, and <code>form.elements.phoneNumber</code> is a
    {{RadioNodeList}} of all three fields.
: Autofill
:: Existing autofill field names already describe common segments:
    <{input/autocomplete/tel-area-code}>, <{input/autocomplete/tel-local-prefix}>, and
    <{input/autocomplete/tel-local-suffix}> for telephone numbers. When a user agent fills a whole
    value into the first segment, such as a <{input/autocomplete/one-time-code}>, [[#distributing-text]]
    spreads it across the chain.
: User agents without support
:: Each segment is submitted as its own entry with the shared name, for example
    <code>phoneNumber=555&amp;phoneNumber=123&amp;phoneNumber=4567</code>. A server that joins repeated
    values for such names accepts submissions from both kinds of user agent.


<h2 id=changes-to-html>Changes to the HTML Standard</h2>

To integrate this specification into the HTML Standard [[!HTML]], make these changes:

1. In <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/input.html#the-input-element">the
    <code>input</code> element</a>:
    * Add <{input/link}> to the element's content attributes.
    * Add <code>HTMLInputElement includes LinkedControlAttributes;</code> to the DOM interface.
    * Add a <{input/link}> row to the table that summarizes which content attributes apply to each
        <{input/type}> state. Mark it "Yes" for the Text, Search, Telephone, and Password states, and
        not applicable for every other state.
    * In the "Bookkeeping details" of the Text and Search, Telephone, and Password states, add
        <{input/link}> to the attributes that apply, and {{LinkedControlAttributes/linkElement}} to
        the IDL attributes that apply. In every other state, add <{input/link}> to the content
        attributes that must not be specified.
1. In <a href="https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-elements.html#the-select-element">the
    <code>select</code> element</a>, add <{select/link}> to the element's content attributes, and
    <code>HTMLSelectElement includes LinkedControlAttributes;</code> to the DOM interface.
1. Apply the change in [[#patch-construct-the-entry-list]].
1. Add the definitions in [[#link-attribute]], [[#user-interaction]], and [[#joined-groups]] as a new
    subsection of "Attributes common to form controls".
1. In the index of element attributes, add a row: attribute <code>link</code>; elements
    <code>input</code>, <code>select</code>; description "Next control in a sequence of linked
    controls"; value "ID".


<h2 id=a11y>Accessibility considerations</h2>

Moving focus while the user is typing is a change of context under
<a href="https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/#on-input">Success Criterion 3.2.2 On Input</a> [[WCAG22]],
unless the user is told about the behavior before using the controls. Some users,
including many screen reader and screen magnifier users, find automatic focus movement disorienting.

* User agents should give users a way to turn off automatic focus advancement. When it is off,
    [=advance focus=] does nothing. Users can still move between controls by typing past the end
    of a full control ([[#distributing-text]]), with the arrow keys and Backspace
    ([[#moving-between-controls]]), or with [=sequential focus navigation=].
* Authors should group linked controls, for example with a <{fieldset}> and <{legend}>. They should
    give each control its own accessible name, and say in the group's label or nearby instructions
    that focus moves automatically.
* User agents should expose link chains to accessibility APIs, so that assistive technology can
    tell users where a control sits in its chain (for example, "area code, 1 of 3").

Issue: Define how link chains map to platform accessibility APIs in [[HTML-AAM-1.0]]. One option is a
"flows to" relationship, as for <code>aria-flowto</code> [[WAI-ARIA-1.2]], together with the position
in the chain and the chain's size.

Because distribution keeps pasting and autofill working, users of voice control, switch access, and
password managers can supply a whole value at once instead of entering it one segment at a time.


<h2 id=security-privacy>Security and privacy considerations</h2>

* This specification exposes no new information. A joined entry contains exactly the values that
    its controls would otherwise submit separately.
* A [=link target=] must be in the same [=tree=] as the element that names it. Links therefore
    can't move focus or text into another document, such as a cross-origin <code>iframe</code>, or
    across a shadow root boundary.
* Focus moves only in response to the user's own editing actions, and only within one document.
    Script can already move focus with <code>focus()</code>.
* [=Distribute text=] stops at any boundary between a <a element-state for=input>Password</a>
    control and a control of another type. Pasted secrets therefore never spill from a masked field
    into a visible one, or the other way around.


<h2 id=more-examples>More examples</h2>

*This section is non-normative.*

<div class=example id=example-country-code>
    A <{select}> element can be part of a chain. When the user picks a country code, focus moves to
    the area code, and the joined value comes out in a full international format.

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <fieldset>
      <legend>Mobile number (focus moves to the next box automatically)</legend>
      <select name="mobile" link="mobileArea" autocomplete="tel-country-code"
              aria-label="Country code">
        <option value="+1">+1</option>
        <option value="+52">+52</option>
      </select>
      <input type="tel" id="mobileArea" name="mobile" maxlength="3" link="mobilePrefix"
             autocomplete="tel-area-code" aria-label="Area code">
      <input type="tel" id="mobilePrefix" name="mobile" maxlength="3" link="mobileLine"
             autocomplete="tel-local-prefix" aria-label="Prefix">
      <input type="tel" id="mobileLine" name="mobile" maxlength="4"
             autocomplete="tel-local-suffix" aria-label="Line number">
    </fieldset>
    </xmp>

    The form submits <code>mobile=%2B15551234567</code>, which is <code>+15551234567</code> once
    percent-decoded.
</div>

<div class=example id=example-one-time-code>
    A one-time code with one box per digit. Pasting <kbd>482913</kbd> into the first box, or autofilling
    it there, fills all six boxes.

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <form action="/verify" method="post">
      <fieldset>
        <legend>Enter the 6-digit code we sent you</legend>
        <input name="code" id="d1" link="d2" maxlength="1" inputmode="numeric"
               autocomplete="one-time-code" aria-label="Digit 1">
        <input name="code" id="d2" link="d3" maxlength="1" inputmode="numeric" aria-label="Digit 2">
        <input name="code" id="d3" link="d4" maxlength="1" inputmode="numeric" aria-label="Digit 3">
        <input name="code" id="d4" link="d5" maxlength="1" inputmode="numeric" aria-label="Digit 4">
        <input name="code" id="d5" link="d6" maxlength="1" inputmode="numeric" aria-label="Digit 5">
        <input name="code" id="d6" maxlength="1" inputmode="numeric" aria-label="Digit 6">
      </fieldset>
      <button>Verify</button>
    </form>
    </xmp>

    The form submits <code>code=482913</code>. If the user notices a wrong digit, they can press
    <kbd>Backspace</kbd> repeatedly to move back through the boxes, or select a digit and type a new
    one.
</div>

<div class=example id=example-card>
    Links and names do different jobs. The last card number group links to the expiry month so focus
    keeps moving, but the card number and the expiry date have different names, so each is its own
    entry.

    <xmp highlight=html>
    <input name="cc-number" id="cc1" link="cc2" maxlength="4" inputmode="numeric"
           autocomplete="cc-number" aria-label="Card number, group 1 of 4">
    <input name="cc-number" id="cc2" link="cc3" maxlength="4" inputmode="numeric"
           aria-label="Card number, group 2 of 4">
    <input name="cc-number" id="cc3" link="cc4" maxlength="4" inputmode="numeric"
           aria-label="Card number, group 3 of 4">
    <input name="cc-number" id="cc4" link="ccMonth" maxlength="4" inputmode="numeric"
           aria-label="Card number, group 4 of 4">
    <select name="cc-exp" id="ccMonth" link="ccYear" autocomplete="cc-exp-month"
            aria-label="Expiry month">
      <option value="01">01</option> <!-- … --> <option value="12">12</option>
    </select>
    <select name="cc-exp" id="ccYear" autocomplete="cc-exp-year" aria-label="Expiry year">
      <option value="26">2026</option> <!-- … --> <option value="35">2035</option>
    </select>
    </xmp>

    Choosing 12 and 2030 submits
    <code>cc-number=4111111111111111&amp;cc-exp=1230</code>. Pasting a 16-digit card number into the
    first group fills all four groups. Distribution stops at the <{select}> element.
</div>


<h2 id=open-issues>Open issues</h2>

Issue: <strong>Attribute name.</strong> <code>link</code> is easy to confuse with the <{link}> element
and with the obsolete <code>link</code> attribute of <code>body</code>. Alternatives include
<code>next</code>, <code>linkto</code>, and <code>continues</code>.

Issue: <strong>Formatting characters in pasted text.</strong> Pasting <kbd>(555) 123-4567</kbd> into
the telephone fields spreads the parentheses, space, and hyphen across the segments. Should user
agents drop such characters when every control in the chain has
<code>inputmode="numeric"</code>, or should authors signal the allowed characters some other way?

Issue: <strong>Separator.</strong> Should authors be able to put a separator between the joined
values, for example with a <code>joiner="-"</code> attribute on the first control?

Issue: <strong>More controls.</strong> Should <{textarea}>, <{input}> elements in the
<a element-state for=input>Number</a>, <a element-state for=input>Email</a>, and
<a element-state for=input>URL</a> states, or <{select}> elements with <{select/multiple}> be
linkable?

Issue: <strong>More editing commands.</strong> Should <kbd>Delete</kbd> at the end of a control, word
deletion, and <kbd>Home</kbd>/<kbd>End</kbd> also cross control boundaries?

Issue: <strong>Validating the whole value.</strong> Should there be a way to validate the joined value
as a whole, for example a pattern that applies to the entire chain?
